Research notes for the implementer — these are notes to help, not a spec. Please still read the issue body and CLAUDE.md yourself and verify anything critical before making changes.

Scope check: I re-read app/app/index.tsx end to end at the two locations named in the issue and both are exactly as quoted, so no line numbers have drifted since the issue was filed.

1. Lines 411-413 (just above `const now = useNow();`):
   ```
   // Wall-clock-aligned 1s tick - fuels the new design's ClockRail / PrimaryCard /
   // QueuedRow render path. The legacy TargetBlock still pulls its `countdown`
   // string from the existing setInterval below; both run independently.
   ```
   This describes a second, independent countdown path driven by a legacy TargetBlock render. That render does not exist. `useNow()` (app/lib/useNow.ts) is the only clock-tick source feeding the render tree now.

2. Lines 2113-2121 (comment block right before `if (!fullScreen) {`):
   ```
   // ─── New-design mobile path (non-fullscreen) ─────────────────────────
   // The visual shell of the redesign lives here; editing still routes back
   // through the legacy TargetBlock for now, rendered below the new cards when
   // a cue is selected (replaced by CueEditModal in step 5).
   // Web now uses the new design too (legacy render below is dead code that
   // we keep around until a follow-up cleanup commit). The `!fullScreen` gate
   // means web-fullscreen falls through to OnAirView via the branch above on
   // native and to legacy on web - but web has no fullscreen entry-point in
   // the new Header, so this never executes.
   ```
   Same problem, worse: it tells a reader to go look at "legacy render below" and to wait for "step 5" — both dead ends. `CueEditModal` (imported app/app/index.tsx:11, rendered at line 2286) is already the sole editing surface, driven by `editingBlockId` / `editingSection` state (declared right above, at lines 403-409).

What the fix is: this is a pure comment rewrite/deletion, nothing else. There is no library, API, or framework involved, so no WebSearch was needed or done — the whole task is internal-repo archaeology. Recommend replacing both blocks with short, accurate prose (per CLAUDE.md: "comments must be short and simple enough for a non-technical person to understand", and no semantic line breaks). For example, something like:
   - At 411-413: a one-liner noting `now` is the single wall-clock tick that drives every countdown display (ClockRail, PrimaryCard, QueuedRow, PassedStrip), no second/independent tick.
   - At 2113-2121: a short block describing this as the non-fullscreen render path shared by native and web, with `CueEditModal` as the one editing surface — drop the "legacy TargetBlock", "step 5", and "kept until cleanup" language entirely.
   Do not just delete the blocks outright without replacement if the surrounding "why" (e.g. why web and native share this branch) is still useful — judge that against CLAUDE.md's "stay minimal" instruction on a per-block basis.

Gotchas / things that could trip this up:
   - `app/components/TargetBlock.tsx` still exists as a file on disk and `TargetBlockType` (the data type, not a component) is imported and used throughout index.tsx (createDefaultBlock, computeAlertFireDate, etc.) — do NOT delete that file or the type import as part of this change. Only the *comments* claiming a `<TargetBlock` component still renders are stale; the type itself is very much alive and load-bearing.
   - This is comment-only, so it cannot affect timing, the countdown, the 3-second beep, alarms, or notification delivery — those are all CLAUDE.md non-negotiables but none of them are touched by a comment edit.
   - Verify after editing with a plain diff (confirm only comment lines changed, no code moved) and optionally `npx tsc --noEmit` inside `app/` — it will pass trivially since comments don't type-check, but it's a cheap sanity check that nothing else shifted.

